INR / Anticoagulation Monitoring
General Practice Other
Established indication for warfarin, Scheduled coagulation monitoring, Dosage adjustment based on target, Assessment of bleeding signs

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-021
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe epistaxis in a patient on warfarin, requiring immediate assessment of bleeding severity and coagulation status.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with an established mechanical heart valve on warfarin experiencing a sudden, heavy nosebleed lasting over 45 minutes that failed to resolve with simple first aid. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to assess for active haemorrhagic shock, secure hemostasis, and perform point-of-care INR testing to rule out critical supratherapeutic coagulopathy. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed a direct risk of exsanguination or massive internal bleeding overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My nose won't stop bleeding no matter how hard I pinch it, and I take warfarin every single day."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an established history of a mechanical heart valve and current therapeutic management with a vitamin K antagonist (warfarin). The patient was due for routine scheduled monitoring of the coagulation profile tomorrow. However, one hour ago, they experienced an unprovoked, heavy anterior epistaxis. A thorough assessment for recent signs of clinical bleeding reveals no haematoma, melaena, or hematuria. An evaluation of recent dietary changes significantly impacting vitamin K intake is negative, but a review of newly prescribed interacting medications reveals the patient commenced a course of oral Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole for a UTI three days ago, known to severely potentiate warfarin.
Functional Status: Independent, currently seated and applying pressure to the nares, distressed by the volume of blood.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of sudden hemodynamic compromise due to active bleeding on systemic anticoagulation.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Anxious, bloodstains on clothing, actively holding a soaked towel to the face.
Physical Exam: ENT: Examination of the nares reveals active, slow oozing from Little's area bilaterally; no massive posterior hemorrhage. Cardiovascular: Normal prosthetic valve clicks auscultated, tachycardic. The clinical pathway mandates point-of-care testing resulting in an unexpectedly high INR (7.5), confirming the need for an immediate dosage adjustment based on the target range (2.5-3.5) and management of the active bleeding. The assessment confirms an established indication for warfarin, scheduled coagulation monitoring overridden by acute bleeding, dosage adjustment requirements, and the assessment of bleeding signs. Expected investigation results include a prolonged international normalized ratio (INR) reflecting a dangerous supratherapeutic state that assists in confirming the iatrogenic coagulopathy.
Environmental Observations: Multiple blood-soaked tissues in the bathroom basin; home is otherwise safe.
Medication Reconciliation: Warfarin 5mg daily verified; Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole 160/800mg BD verified (offending interacting agent).

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: INR / Anticoagulation Monitoring (Supratherapeutic INR with Active Epistaxis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Posterior Epistaxis requiring surgical ligation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bleeding is visibly originating from the anterior septum and is slowing with direct pressure, without massive blood flowing down the posterior oropharynx.

Undiagnosed Malignancy (Nasal) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a massive, known iatrogenic coagulopathy directly explaining the bleeding in an otherwise anatomically normal nasal passage.

Severe Symptomatic Anaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintained blood pressure, absence of severe pallor or postural syncope, despite the visible blood loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Bleeding Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a prolonged, significant hemorrhage directly linked to the initiation of a highly interacting antibiotic while on systemic anticoagulation. Telephone triage accurately flagged the risk of a critically high INR causing life-threatening bleeding. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to apply topical vasoconstrictors/pressure to arrest the epistaxis, perform an immediate point-of-care INR test (resulting in 7.5), and immediately cease both the warfarin and the interacting antibiotic. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have allowed the supratherapeutic state to worsen, risking catastrophic spontaneous intracranial or gastrointestinal hemorrhage overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Applied topical adrenaline/lidocaine soaked cotton to the anterior septum with firm pressure for 15 minutes, achieving total hemostasis. Withheld warfarin doses for tonight and tomorrow. Ceased the Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole. Administered oral Vitamin K 1mg stat to gently reverse the severe coagulopathy without completely clotting the mechanical valve.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent future dangerous drug interactions, commit to showing your physical warfarin blister pack to any doctor or pharmacist every single time a new medication is prescribed. Keep the physical warfarin pack at the very front of your wallet or purse. This ensures an unavoidable visual reminder during any medical consultation, forcing an interaction check before you swallow a new, potentially life-threatening pill.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory urgent laboratory INR draw tomorrow morning and GP review to adjust the ongoing dosage.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if the nosebleed restarts and fails to stop after 20 minutes of firm, continuous pinching, if you vomit blood, pass black tarry stools, or develop a sudden severe headache.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The bleeding has stopped. The small dose of Vitamin K will slowly bring your blood thickness back to a safe level over the next 24 hours without putting your heart valve at risk.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate emergency medical care if you develop any signs of internal bleeding tonight, such as severe, sudden abdominal pain or sudden dizziness upon standing.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The new antibiotic you started completely changed how your body processes warfarin, making your blood dangerously thin and causing the nosebleed. We have stopped both medications temporarily to let your blood recover.
Actionable Steps: Do not take your warfarin tonight or tomorrow. Go directly to the pathology lab first thing tomorrow morning for a formal blood draw, then see your regular doctor.

